AI-VOX3: trace every Wi-Fi init allocation so the -12 root cause is visible
===========================================================================

WHY THIS PATCH EXISTS
=====================

esp32s3_wlan_sta_initialize() still fails with -12 (-ENOMEM) after 0012
(verified on real hardware, run#137 / run#138).  run#138 proved the failure is
NOT total-heap exhaustion:

    [BSP] alloc[pre-wlan]:  1MB=OK 4KB=OK      <- 8MB PSRAM is in the heap
    [BSP] -> wlan_sta_initialize
    [BSP] <- wlan_sta_initialize ret=-12        <- but Wi-Fi still starves
    [BSP] alloc[post-wlan]: 1MB=OK 4KB=OK

So the failing request is either (a) an INTERNAL-SRAM-only allocation that the
allocator can no longer satisfy contiguously, or (b) a task/queue creation
failure inside the pre-compiled IDF Wi-Fi library.  Both are invisible today:

  * the library reports failure through wifi_osi_funcs_t callbacks, and
  * its own logs are dropped by esp_log_writev_wrapper() because
    max_level = ESP_LOG_NONE unless CONFIG_DEBUG_WIRELESS_{INFO,WARN,ERROR}
    is defined -- and that symbol exists in NO Kconfig of this tree, so it
    can never be enabled from a defconfig (olddefconfig silently ignores it).

This patch turns the lights on, purely additive, zero behaviour change:

  1. esp_log_write_wrapper / esp_log_writev_wrapper: max_level ERROR instead
     of NONE (CONFIG_DEBUG_WIRELESS_ERROR still wins when it is ever defined).
  2. A g_wifi_alloc_trace flag, enabled only around esp_wifi_init() inside
     esp_wifi_adapter_init(), so no runtime cost outside Wi-Fi bring-up.
  3. Trace lines ([WA] prefix, ets_printf -- syslog/nerr does NOT reach the
     console on this build) in every allocation / task / queue callback the
     IDF library can call:
        esp_malloc_internal, esp_calloc_internal, esp_zalloc_internal,
        esp_realloc_internal, esp_malloc, esp_wifi_malloc, esp_wifi_calloc,
        esp_wifi_zalloc, esp_wifi_realloc, esp_wifi_create_queue,
        esp_queue_create, esp_semphr_create, esp_mutex_create,
        esp_task_create_pinned_to_core
     Each line prints the requested size, the returned pointer, and whether it
     came from internal SRAM (INT) or PSRAM (PSRAM), or FAILED.
  4. esp_wifi_init() (esp32s3_wireless.c): phase markers, so if every
     allocation succeeds we still learn WHICH of coex_init /
     esp_wifi_init_internal / esp_phy_modem_init / esp_supplicant_init
     returns the error.

EXPECTED OUTCOME (run#139)
==========================

Either one callback prints FAIL with a size -- that size is the internal-SRAM
requirement Wi-Fi cannot meet, and the fix follows directly (reduce the
matching CONFIG_ESP32S3_WIFI_* buffer count, or free internal DRAM); or all
allocations succeed and a phase marker prints FAILED -- then the problem is
inside the library proper and we attack it from a different angle.

To be removed once the root cause is fixed.

